{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Completion-recovery context bound conflicts with the verifier's own remediation advice\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nClosing story 2932 hit:\n\n```\ncannot close 2932-8a98-6d70-4550: completion verification could not run\n(step 'decide@then/verify' failed: completion recovery context bound exceeded).\nThe ticket's context (41,595 chars) exceeds the bounded completion-recovery limit (24,000 chars),\nso the recovery pass was refused before it ran — shorten the ticket's description/comments.\n```\n\nMEASURED composition of that ticket at the time: description **21,728** chars, 8 comments totalling **19,410** chars (41,138 total).\n\nTwo things make this more than a one-off size complaint:\n\n1. **The description ALONE was 21,728 of the 24,000 budget.** Even with every comment deleted, that ticket has ~2.3k chars of headroom for its entire evidence history. For a plan of this size the recovery pass is effectively unreachable, permanently.\n\n2. **The bound fights the verifier's own remediation instruction.** When a criterion is unmet the verifier says: *'add a comment to this ticket that documents the evidence — cite the concrete artifacts...'*. Following that advice is what pushed 2932 over the limit: a first close attempt failed on ONE unmet [operator-attested] criterion (correctly — its evidence named a Verified +1 from a superseded patchset), and the ~3.5k-char comment recording the current evidence is what tipped it past the bound. So the gate asked for evidence and then refused to run because the evidence was added.\n\n## Reproduction / observed behaviour\n\n- attempt 1: main verification RAN and returned a substantive per-criterion verdict (1 unmet).\n- attempt 2 (after adding the evidence comment): refused at `decide@then/verify` on the recovery bound.\n- `rebar compact <id>` does NOT help — it snapshots the event log, leaving description+comment TEXT unchanged (verified: 41,138 before and after).\n- attempt 3: SUCCEEDED and the ticket closed, with no content change between attempts 2 and 3.\n\nThat last point matters: the failure is **NOT deterministic** on ticket size. It fires only when the recovery path is entered (the main pass has a different, larger budget). So the same ticket can fail and then pass with identical content, which makes it read as a flake while actually being a real bound.\n\n## Why the workarounds are bad\n\n- **Shorten the description** — the description IS the plan-review-attested material. Editing it changes the material fingerprint and invalidates the attestation, which `require_plan_review_for_close` then demands be re-earned. So the remedy for a close-gate problem is to break the other close-gate precondition.\n- **`--force-close`** — discards the completion gate entirely for exactly the large, complex tickets where it is most valuable, and AGENTS.md rightly frames force as an operator judgment call rather than a routine move.\n- **Retry until it passes** — what actually worked here, but it is cargo-culting a nondeterministic bound.\n\n## Candidate directions (not a decision)\n\n- Give the recovery pass the same budget as the main verification pass, or derive it from the model's real context window rather than a fixed 24,000.\n- Budget the recovery input by TRUNCATING/summarizing oldest comments rather than refusing the whole pass — a bounded pass over recent evidence beats no pass.\n- Prioritise the evidence the verifier itself asked for (most recent comments) over the oldest history when packing that budget.\n- At minimum: make the error distinguish 'this ticket can never fit' (description alone exceeds budget) from 'this attempt did not fit', and stop advising a fix (\"shorten the description\") that invalidates the plan attestation.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] a test constructs a ticket whose description alone approaches the recovery budget and asserts the completion gate still produces a verdict (pass or fail) rather than refusing to run\n- [ ] the recovery pass's budget is documented alongside the main verification pass's, with the relationship between them stated\n- [ ] the error message no longer recommends shortening the description without noting that doing so invalidates the plan-review attestation\n- [ ] [operator-attested] a FIXTURE ticket sized like 2932 (~21.7k description, ~19.4k comments) produces a\n      completion verdict on 3 consecutive attempts, evidenced by the three run outputs in a comment.\n      NOTE: worded against a fixture because ticket 2932 is already CLOSED and cannot be closed again —\n      the original wording named an artifact that can no longer exist."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785450643706915001,"uuid":"637853f8-096d-4598-9523-63f284fa6745"}